<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<TcPlcObject Version="1.1.0.1">
  <GVL Name="PMPS_PARAM" Id="{a73b5237-920e-4906-b8ec-4aaa7f8c0e45}" ParameterList="True">
    <Declaration><![CDATA[(*
    PMPS_PARAM : deployment limits for the PMPS fast-fault and beam-parameter
    pools, and the transmission margin used by the beam-parameter comparison.

    Purpose:
        Collect, in one place, the numeric limits that size the PMPS pools and
        that decide how much transmission slack the beam-parameter comparison
        tolerates, so an installation is sized for its own machine instead of
        the values being hard-coded in the blocks that consume them:
          - MAX_FAST_FAULTS sizes a fast-fault output's registration pool and
            the FFO logger's cumulative min-time-violation budget.
          - MAX_ASSERTIONS sizes the arbiter's beam-parameter request pool
            (and the assertion array published to EPICS) and is the capacity
            the BPTM tests before it asserts a new transition.
          - TRANS_MARGIN is the fraction above the requested transmission
            level that F_SafeBPCompare still accepts as safe.

    Usage:
        Refer to the values as PMPS_PARAM.MAX_FAST_FAULTS,
        PMPS_PARAM.MAX_ASSERTIONS and PMPS_PARAM.TRANS_MARGIN; the
        qualified_only attribute below forbids unqualified access, so every use
        site names the GVL explicitly. All three are CONSTANT and are therefore
        compiled into the blocks that read them: changing one resizes the
        arrays derived from it and needs a rebuild and download, not a live
        write. None of them is EPICS-writable - the arrays they size are
        published by their owning blocks instead
        (FB_HardwareFFOutput.astFF, FB_BeamParamAssertionPool.epicsDataPool).

    Objectives:
        - One source of truth for pool capacity and the transmission margin,
          so the consuming blocks cannot disagree about either.
        - Keep the limits tunable per installation without editing logic.
        - Keep TRANS_MARGIN reviewed together with
          PMPS_GVL.TRANS_SCALING_FACTOR: the margin is added to the scaling
          factor, so a new scaling factor invalidates the margin and the
          TRANS_MARGIN comment must be re-checked.

    History:
        2020-09-09  Alex Wallace     : Created - parameterized the arbiter and FF
                                       pool sizes so they can be customized per
                                       application (MAX_FAST_FAULTS, then DINT,
                                       changed to UINT on 2020-09-17;
                                       MAX_ASSERTIONS = 20).
        2020-11-05  Alex Wallace     : Margin constant added with the transmission
                                       compare - a 5% margin above the defined
                                       device thresholds.
        2021-04-23  ghalym           : Margin carried as REAL#0.05 (unit test
                                       fixes).
        2025-04-22  Kaushik Malapati : TRANS_MARGIN raised from REAL#0.05 to
                                       REAL#0.20.
        2026-11-27  Audit fix        : Header added for audit finding #1128; no
                                       constant, type or value changed.
*)
{attribute 'qualified_only'}
VAR_GLOBAL CONSTANT
    MAX_FAST_FAULTS	:	UINT := 50; // Max fast faults for an FFO
    MAX_ASSERTIONS    :    UDINT := 20; //Maximum number of BP requests in the arbiter
    TRANS_MARGIN : REAL := REAL#0.20; // Allowed % margin above requested transmission level in SafeBPCompare (0.2000 = 20deci% default). Note: change this value if scaling factor changes.
END_VAR]]></Declaration>
  </GVL>
</TcPlcObject>